## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-hacking techniques across a 128-bit value by splitting into two 64-bit halves and summing, without loops or branches.

## Key Concepts

- 64-bit popcount via parallel bit counting masks
- Treat 128-bit as two 64-bit blocks
- No loops or branches; purely bitwise ops

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7f;
}
unsigned int countBits128(uint128 x){
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage __builtin_popcountll if available?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a new angle: 128-bit popcount via per-half 64-bit parallel counting using a fixed, loopless sequence of masks.

## Key Concepts

- Bit-twiddling for popcount
- Handling 128-bit values by splitting into 64-bit halves
- No branches or loops; purely arithmetic masks

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint32_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.lo) + popcount64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for 256-bit using the same pattern? 
- What are the trade-offs of inlining vs. a dedicated intrinsic on your compiler/architecture?

## Why This Is Asked
Reversing 128 bits tests a candidate's ability to decompose 128-bit data into two 64-bit lanes and apply a branch-free bit-reversal at the lower level.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value represented as {hi, lo}
- constant-time 64-bit bit-reversal using masks
- composing to achieve full 128-bit reversal without branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function reverse64(v){
  v = ((v >>> 1) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((v & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  v = ((v >>> 2) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((v & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  v = ((v >>> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) | ((v & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn) << 4n);
  v = ((v >>> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) | ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn) << 8n);
  v = ((v >>> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) | ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn) << 16n);
  v = (v >>> 32) | (v << 32);
  return v;
}
function reverseBits128(x){
  // x.hi, x.lo are 64-bit values represented as BigInt
  return { hi: reverse64(x.lo), lo: reverse64(x.hi) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type if provided?
- What are the trade-offs of using 64-bit reversals vs a single 128-bit table lookup on platforms with broad SIMD support?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of 64-bit bit reversals and cross-half manipulation without loops. It ensures ability to compose operations on 128-bit types.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit bit reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct handling
- Non-branching composition

## Code Example
```javascript
// Outline only: reverse64 implemented with a sequence of mask/shifts
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1 | (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2 | (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) << 4 | (x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  x = ((x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x << 48) | ((x & 0xffff) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0xffff);
  return x;
}
```

